    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org//Childbearing-ageWomenwithEpilepsyOntology#Cerebral_Malaria -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org//Childbearing-ageWomenwithEpilepsyOntology#Cerebral_Malaria">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/administrator/ontologies/2022/0/untitled-ontology-34#OWLClass258bbb8e_6ff9_4067_b26d_072c666fb21d"/>
        <Literature>Ssentongo P. Prevalence and incidence of new-onset seizures and epilepsy in patients with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV): Systematic review and meta-analysis. Epilepsy Behav. 2019;93:49-55. doi:10.1016/j.yebeh.2019.01.033</Literature>
        <untitled-ontology-34:Chinese>脑型疟疾</untitled-ontology-34:Chinese>
        <untitled-ontology-34:MESH_Definition>A condition characterized by somnolence or coma in the presence of an acute infection with PLASMODIUM FALCIPARUM (and rarely other Plasmodium species). Initial clinical manifestations include HEADACHES; SEIZURES; and alterations of mentation followed by a rapid progression to COMA. Pathologic features include cerebral capillaries filled with parasitized erythrocytes and multiple small foci of cortical and subcortical necrosis. (From Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, p136)</untitled-ontology-34:MESH_Definition>
        <untitled-ontology-34:NCIt_Definition>A sequestration of Plasmodium falciparum in the brain, which can cause coma and/or seizures.</untitled-ontology-34:NCIt_Definition>
        <untitled-ontology-34:Synonyms>Cerebral Malaria
Malaria Meningitis
Meningitis, Malaria
Malaria, Cerebral
Cerebral malaria (disorder)
Cerebral malaria NOS</untitled-ontology-34:Synonyms>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org//Childbearing-ageWomenwithEpilepsyOntology#Cerebral_cysticercosis -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org//Childbearing-ageWomenwithEpilepsyOntology#Cerebral_cysticercosis">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/administrator/ontologies/2022/0/untitled-ontology-34#OWLClass258bbb8e_6ff9_4067_b26d_072c666fb21d"/>
        <Literature>Stelzle D, Schmidt V, Keller L, et al. Characteristics of people with epilepsy and Neurocysticercosis in three eastern African countries-A pooled analysis [published correction appears in PLoS Negl Trop Dis. 2023 Jan 26;17(1):e0011101]. PLoS Negl Trop Dis. 2022;16(11):e0010870. Published 2022 Nov 7. doi:10.1371/journal.pntd.0010870</Literature>
        <untitled-ontology-34:Chinese>脑囊虫病</untitled-ontology-34:Chinese>
        <untitled-ontology-34:MESH_Definition>Infection of the brain, spinal cord, or perimeningeal structures with the larval forms of the genus TAENIA (primarily T. solium in humans). Lesions formed by the organism are referred to as cysticerci. The infection may be subacute or chronic, and the severity of symptoms depends on the severity of the host immune response and the location and number of lesions. SEIZURES represent the most common clinical manifestation although focal neurologic deficits may occur. (From Joynt, Clinical Neurology, 1998, Ch27, pp46-50)</untitled-ontology-34:MESH_Definition>
        <untitled-ontology-34:Synonyms>Neurocysticercoses
Neurocoenurosis
Neurocoenuroses
Central Nervous System Cysticercosis
CNS Cysticercosis
CNS Cysticercoses
Cysticercosis, CNS
Cysticercosis, Central Nervous System
Cysticercosis, Brain
Brain Cysticercosis
Cerebral Coenurosis
Cerebral Coenuroses
Coenurosis, Cerebral
Cerebral Cysticercosis
Cerebral Cysticercoses
Cysticercosis, Cerebral</untitled-ontology-34:Synonyms>
    </owl:Class>
    


