    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org//Childbearing-ageWomenwithEpilepsyOntology#Gonadotropins -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org//Childbearing-ageWomenwithEpilepsyOntology#Gonadotropins">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org//Childbearing-ageWomenwithEpilepsyOntology#Hormones"/>
        <Literature>Maguire MJ, Nevitt SJ. Treatments for seizures in catamenial (menstrual-related) epilepsy. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2021;9(9):CD013225. Published 2021 Sep 16. doi:10.1002/14651858.CD013225.pub3</Literature>
        <untitled-ontology-34:Chinese>促性腺激素</untitled-ontology-34:Chinese>
        <untitled-ontology-34:MESH_Definition>Hormones that stimulate gonadal functions such as GAMETOGENESIS and sex steroid hormone production in the OVARY and the TESTIS. Major gonadotropins are glycoproteins produced primarily by the adenohypophysis (GONADOTROPINS, PITUITARY) and the placenta (CHORIONIC GONADOTROPIN). In some species, pituitary PROLACTIN and PLACENTAL LACTOGEN exert some luteotropic activities.</untitled-ontology-34:MESH_Definition>
        <untitled-ontology-34:NCIt_Definition>Heterodimeric glycoprotein Gonadotropin consists of alpha (common)/beta (unique) protein subunits. The alpha subunit is present in other heterodimeric glycoprotein hormones. The beta subunit confers biological specificity and stimulates ovarian synthesis of steroids (progesterone) to maintain pregnancy.</untitled-ontology-34:NCIt_Definition>
        <untitled-ontology-34:Synonyms>Gonadotropin [EPC]
gonadotropins
gonadotropin
Substance with gonadotropin mechanism of action (substance)</untitled-ontology-34:Synonyms>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org//Childbearing-ageWomenwithEpilepsyOntology#Guanyuan_Acupoint -->

